##  Working Principle

The potentiometer produces a variable analog voltage (0–5V), which Arduino reads via its **10-bit ADC** (0–1023).
This value is **mapped to a delay period (50ms – 500ms)**, which controls the rise and fall timing of the signal — producing a **triangle-shaped waveform** on the Serial Plotter.
Simultaneously, **Pin D9 outputs a digital HIGH/LOW** signal, which can be probed physically.

Higher pot value → larger delay → lower frequency
Lower pot value  → smaller delay → higher frequency


##  Output

Serial Plotter displays a **clean triangle wave**, oscillating between 0 and 1023, with frequency varying as the potentiometer is adjusted.

| Pot Position | Signal Frequency |
|-------------|-----------------|
| Minimum     | High frequency  |
| Mid         | Medium frequency|
| Maximum     | Low frequency   |

